1. What is Patch Management?

Patch Management is the process of identifying, testing, installing, and verifying software updates (patches) on a Linux system to fix security vulnerabilities, bugs, and improve performance.

2. What is a Patch?

A patch is a small software update released by the software vendor to improve or fix an existing application or operating system.

3. Real-Life Example

Imagine your car manufacturer finds that the brake system has a problem.

Instead of giving you a new car, they replace only the faulty brake part.

This replacement is similar to a patch.

4. Types of Patches
a) Security Patch

Fixes security vulnerabilities.

Example:

A vulnerability is discovered in OpenSSH.

The vendor releases a security patch.

Install it immediately to prevent attacks.

b) Bug Fix Patch

Fixes software errors.

Example:

Firefox crashes when opening certain websites.

The vendor releases a patch to resolve the issue

c) Performance Patch

Improves software speed or efficiency.

Example:

A database application is consuming excessive memory.

The vendor releases a patch to optimize memory usage.

d) Feature Patch

Adds small new features or enhancements.

Example:

A new command-line option is added to an existing tool.

